WEBVTT

NOTE whisper small.en

00:00:00.020 --> 00:00:05.620
In the previous video, you saw the basic blocks of implementing a deep neural network, a forward

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.220
propagation step for each layer and a corresponding backward propagation step.

00:00:10.220 --> 00:00:12.540
Let's see how you can actually implement these steps.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:14.460
We'll start with forward propagation.

00:00:14.460 --> 00:00:21.460
Recall that what this will do is input AL minus 1 and output AL and the cache ZL.

00:00:21.460 --> 00:00:27.220
And we just said that from an implementation point of view, maybe we'll cache WL and

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:31.820
BL as well, just to make the functions call a bit easier in the programming exercise.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:35.700
And so the equations for this should already look familiar.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:44.940
The way to implement the forward function is just this, equals WL times AL minus 1 plus

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:53.940
BL and then AL equals the activation function applied to Z.

00:00:53.940 --> 00:01:06.100
And if you want a vectorized implementation, then it's just that times AL minus 1 plus B

00:01:06.100 --> 00:01:15.780
with the B, adding B being a Python broadcasting, and AL equals G applied element-wise to Z.

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:21.660
And you remember on the diagram for the forward step, where we had this chain of boxes going

00:01:21.660 --> 00:01:29.260
forward, so you initialize that with feeding in A0, which is equal to X.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:33.300
So you initialize this, really, what is the input to the first one?

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:40.500
It's really A0, which is the input features to either for one training example, if you're

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:46.340
doing one example at a time, or A, capital Z, or the entire training set, if you are

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:48.380
processing the entire training set.

00:01:48.380 --> 00:01:53.220
So that's the input to the first forward function in the chain, and then just repeating this

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.380
allows you to compute forward propagation from left to right.

00:01:57.380 --> 00:02:00.580
Next let's talk about the backward propagation step.

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:08.180
Here you go with the input DAL and output DAL minus 1 and DWL and DB.

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.260
Let me just write out the steps you need to compute these things.

00:02:13.020 --> 00:02:25.260
DZL is equal to DAL, element-wise product with G of L prime Z of L.

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:34.820
And then compute the derivatives, DWL equals DZL times A of L minus 1.

00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:39.660
I didn't explicitly put that in the cache, but it turns out you need this as well.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:59.420
And then DBL is equal to DZL, and finally DA of L minus 1 is equal to WL transpose times DZL.

00:02:59.420 --> 00:03:03.180
And I don't want to go through the detailed derivation for this, but it turns out that

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.260
if you take this definition for DA and plug it in here, then you get the same formula

00:03:08.260 --> 00:03:15.500
as we had in the previous week for how you compute DZL as a function of the previous DZL.

00:03:15.500 --> 00:03:26.300
In fact, well if I just plug that in here, you end up that DZL is equal to WL plus 1 transpose

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:34.020
DZL plus 1 times GL prime Z of L.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:36.260
I know this looks like a lot of algebra.

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.580
You could actually double check for yourself that this is the equation we had written down

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:45.100
for backpropagation last week, when we were doing a neural network with just a single hidden

00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:46.100
layer.

00:03:46.100 --> 00:03:51.180
And as a reminder, this times this element-wise product, but so all you need is those four

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:55.020
equations to implement your backward function.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:58.900
And then finally, I'll just write out the vectorized version.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:11.420
So the first line becomes DZL equals DA L element-wise product with GL prime of ZL.

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:13.100
Maybe no surprise there.

00:04:13.100 --> 00:04:23.260
DWL becomes 1 over MDZL times AL minus 1 transpose.

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:32.860
And then DBL becomes 1 over MP dot sum DZL.

00:04:32.860 --> 00:04:35.700
Then X is equal to 1.

00:04:35.700 --> 00:04:37.820
Keep DIMS equals true.

00:04:37.820 --> 00:04:44.620
We talked about the use of MP dot sum in the previous week to compute DB.

00:04:44.620 --> 00:04:56.580
And then finally, DA L minus 1 is WL transpose times DZL.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:08.820
So this allows you to input this quantity DA over here and output DWLDBL, the derivatives

00:05:08.820 --> 00:05:16.160
you need, as well as DA L minus 1 as follows.

00:05:16.160 --> 00:05:19.060
So that's how you implement the backward function.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:23.600
So just to summarize, take the input X.

00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:29.720
You might have the first layer, maybe has a relu activation function, then go to the

00:05:29.720 --> 00:05:36.000
second layer, maybe uses another relu activation function, goes to the third layer, maybe has

00:05:36.000 --> 00:05:42.400
a sigmoid activation function if you're doing binary classification, and this outputs Y-hat.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:46.520
And then using Y-hat, you can compute the loss.

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:49.440
And this allows you to start your backward iteration.

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:51.760
I'll draw the arrows first.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:54.520
I guess I don't have to change pens too much.

00:05:54.520 --> 00:06:03.100
Where you would then have backprop compute the derivatives.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:16.700
Compute, you know, DW3, DB3, DW2, DB2, DW1, DB1.

00:06:16.700 --> 00:06:25.540
And along the way, you would be computing, I guess the cache would transfer Z1, Z2, Z3.

00:06:25.540 --> 00:06:33.340
And here you pass backward DA2 and DA1.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:38.060
This could compute DA0, but we won't use that, so you can just discard that.

00:06:38.060 --> 00:06:44.100
And so this is how you implement forward prop and back prop for a three-layer neural network.

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:48.820
Now there's just one last detail that I didn't talk about, which is for the forward recursion,

00:06:48.820 --> 00:06:52.420
we would initialize it with the input data X.

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.460
How about the backward recursion?

00:06:54.460 --> 00:07:01.460
Well it turns out that DA of L, when you're using logistic regression, when you're doing

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:10.140
binary classification, is equal to Y over A plus 1 minus Y over 1 minus A.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:14.220
So it turns out that the derivative of the loss function with respect to the output with

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:17.220
respect to Y hat can be shown to be equal to this.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:22.220
If you're familiar with calculus, if you look up the loss function L and take derivatives

00:07:22.220 --> 00:07:26.620
with respect to Y hat or with respect to A, you can show that you get that formula.

00:07:26.620 --> 00:07:31.140
So this is the formula you should use for DA for the final layer, capital L.

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:37.300
And of course if you were to have a vectorized implementation, then you initialize the backward

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:45.260
recursion not with this but with DA, capital A, for the layer L, which is going to be the

00:07:45.260 --> 00:07:53.220
same thing for the different examples over A for the first training example plus 1 minus

00:07:53.220 --> 00:07:54.220
Y.

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:59.180
For the first training example over 1 minus A, for the first training example, dot, dot,

00:07:59.180 --> 00:08:02.820
dot, down to the M training example.

00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.580
So 1 minus A of M.

00:08:05.580 --> 00:08:09.620
So that's how you'd implement the vectorized version.

00:08:09.620 --> 00:08:13.200
That's how you initialize the vectorized version of backpropagation.

00:08:13.200 --> 00:08:20.100
So you've now seen the basic building blocks of both forward propagation as well as backpropagation.

00:08:20.100 --> 00:08:24.820
Now if you implement these equations, you will get a correct implementation of forward

00:08:24.820 --> 00:08:27.760
prop and back prop to get you the derivatives you need.

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:29.620
You might be thinking, wow, that was a lot of equations.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:30.620
I'm slightly confused.

00:08:30.620 --> 00:08:32.220
I'm not quite sure I see how this works.

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:36.420
And if you're feeling that way, my advice is when you get to this week's programming

00:08:36.420 --> 00:08:41.180
assignment, you will be able to implement these for yourself and they'll be much more

00:08:41.180 --> 00:08:42.180
concrete.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:45.740
And I know there was a lot of equations and maybe some of the equations didn't make complete

00:08:45.740 --> 00:08:51.060
sense but if you work through the calculus and the linear algebra, which is not easy,

00:08:51.060 --> 00:08:55.260
so feel free to try, but that's actually one of the more difficult derivations in

00:08:55.260 --> 00:08:56.460
machine learning.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.400
It turns out the equations wrote down are just the calculus equations for computing

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:03.000
the derivatives, especially in backprop.

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:06.480
But once again, if this feels a little bit abstract, a little bit mysterious to you,

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:10.000
my advice is when you've done the programming exercise, it will feel a bit more

00:09:10.000 --> 00:09:11.320
concrete to you.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:17.080
Although I have to say, even today when I implement a learning algorithm, sometimes,

00:09:17.080 --> 00:09:20.720
even I'm surprised when my learning algorithm implementation works and it's because

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.840
lot of the complexity of machine learning comes from the data rather than from the

00:09:24.840 --> 00:09:25.840
lines of code.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:29.720
So sometimes you feel like you implement a few lines of code, not quite sure what

00:09:29.720 --> 00:09:31.480
it did, but it almost magically works.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:35.360
And because a lot of the magic is actually not in the piece of code you write,

00:09:35.360 --> 00:09:37.160
which is often not too long.

00:09:37.160 --> 00:09:42.360
It's not exactly simple, but it's not 10,000 or 100,000 lines of code.

00:09:42.360 --> 00:09:45.640
But you feed it so much data that sometimes, even though I've worked in

00:09:45.640 --> 00:09:49.200
machine learning for a long time, sometimes it still surprises me a bit

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.520
when my learning algorithm works because a lot of the complexity of your

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:58.160
learning algorithm comes from the data rather than necessarily from your writing

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:01.160
algorithm, thousands and thousands of lines of code.

00:10:01.160 --> 00:10:02.160
All right.

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.000
So that's how you implement deep neural networks.

00:10:06.000 --> 00:10:08.880
In the game, this will become more concrete when you've done the programming

